# Signing in, and what the scanner makes of it.
#
# Both routes hand back a session cookie carrying `HttpOnly` and `SameSite`, so these pass. Delete
# `HttpOnly;` from `server.mjs` and the second assertion in each test goes red with a **critical**
# `sec/cookie-not-httponly` — which is the point of having the severity matcher here rather than a
# hand-written header assertion: nobody had to know in advance which weakness to look for.
#
# The page heading is `Sign in to The Coffee Shelf` and the button is `Sign in`. That is not
# decoration: `expect text "Sign in"` matched **both** and tflw refused the test as an ambiguous
# locator rather than picking one. The example keeps the distinct heading so the assertion names
# one thing.

@identity @functional
test "signing in over the API hands back a session, and the session is judged"
  api POST /login body { email: "sam@example.com", password: "hunter2" }
  expect status equals 200
  # The severity matcher is what puts this test behind the SCANS door — not the tag beside it.
  # `serious` is a floor, so a critical finding counts too.
  expect response has no serious security violations

@identity @functional
test "signing in on the page is judged the same way the API call is"
  api POST /login body { email: "sam@example.com", password: "hunter2" }
  open "/signin"
  expect text "Sign in to The Coffee Shelf" is visible
  expect response has no serious security violations
